"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,610\nThe Learning Brain. Series 8: Assessment That Teaches\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,235\nEpisode 1\n\n3\n00:00:07,315 --> 00:00:09,210\nTests that teach\n\n4\n00:00:12,010 --> 00:00:14,801\nWe usually think of a test as a measuring stick.\n\n5\n00:00:15,307 --> 00:00:18,877\nLearning happens first, and the test checks it afterwards.\n\n6\n00:00:19,149 --> 00:00:22,763\nBut what if the test itself is where much of the learning happens?\n\n7\n00:00:22,933 --> 00:00:30,083\nIn 2006, Henry Roediger and Jeffrey Karpicke had college students read short science passages.\n\n8\n00:00:30,513 --> 00:00:33,055\nThen students either read the passage again,\n\n9\n00:00:33,289 --> 00:00:36,181\nor tried to write down everything they remembered,\n\n10\n00:00:36,201 --> 00:00:37,365\nwith no feedback.\n\n11\n00:00:37,535 --> 00:00:42,888\nFive minutes later, rereading looked better: eighty one percent of ideas recalled,\n\n12\n00:00:43,023 --> 00:00:44,482\nversus seventy five.\n\n13\n00:00:45,002 --> 00:00:46,911\nBut a week later the pattern flipped.\n\n14\n00:00:47,241 --> 00:00:50,872\nStudents who had tested themselves recalled fifty six percent.\n\n15\n00:00:51,292 --> 00:00:53,796\nThose who reread recalled forty two.\n\n16\n00:00:53,966 --> 00:00:55,387\nThat was not a one off.\n\n17\n00:00:55,762 --> 00:01:04,377\nIn 2017, Olusola Adesope and colleagues pooled one hundred eighteen articles with more than fifteen thousand participants.\n\n18\n00:01:04,841 --> 00:01:08,480\nPractice tests beat rereading and every other comparison,\n\n19\n00:01:08,641 --> 00:01:10,573\nin labs and in classrooms,\n\n20\n00:01:10,792 --> 00:01:13,053\nfor retention and for transfer.\n\n21\n00:01:13,223 --> 00:01:14,258\nWhy does it work?\n\n22\n00:01:14,635 --> 00:01:17,812\nPulling knowledge out of memory strengthens the route back to it.\n\n23\n00:01:18,152 --> 00:01:20,791\nIt also shows students what they don't yet know,\n\n24\n00:01:20,934 --> 00:01:22,630\nwhich rereading hides.\n\n25\n00:01:22,800 --> 00:01:23,907\nThere is a catch.\n\n26\n00:01:24,195 --> 00:01:26,104\nRereading feels more effective.\n\n27\n00:01:26,558 --> 00:01:28,316\nIn the 2006 study,\n\n28\n00:01:28,393 --> 00:01:30,765\nstudents who reread were more confident,\n\n29\n00:01:31,028 --> 00:01:32,888\neven though they remembered less.\n\n30\n00:01:33,236 --> 00:01:36,259\nSo students rarely choose testing on their own,\n\n31\n00:01:36,267 --> 00:01:38,206\nas we saw in Series four.\n\n32\n00:01:38,376 --> 00:01:39,197\nFor teaching.\n\n33\n00:01:39,555 --> 00:01:41,874\nBuild short practice tests into every week,\n\n34\n00:01:41,894 --> 00:01:43,302\nand call them practice.\n\n35\n00:01:43,644 --> 00:01:45,986\nUse questions that make students recall,\n\n36\n00:01:46,006 --> 00:01:47,281\nnot just recognize.\n\n37\n00:01:47,574 --> 00:01:50,804\nExplain the evidence, so students understand why.\n\n38\n00:01:51,256 --> 00:01:55,420\nOne tip to share with students: quiz yourself instead of rereading.\n\n39\n00:01:55,590 --> 00:01:57,629\nA test is not only a measurement.\n\n40\n00:01:57,986 --> 00:02:00,035\nIt is one of the best ways to learn.\n\n"